### Notices Route to the Alerts Topic, Never a New One

**Rule.** A user-facing message that belongs to an existing conversation goes THERE. A user-facing message that belongs to NO existing conversation topic — an alert, a system notice, a housekeeping escalation — routes to the ONE dedicated alerts/hub topic. Creating a NEW Telegram topic per alert, event, or item is forbidden. The only topics that get created are user-initiated ones and a small fixed set of bounded, create-once system topics.

**In practice.** The default destination for an ownerless notice IS the alerts/hub topic (this agent: 7848) — routing there is the RULE, not a fallback the code happens to reach. Since 2026-07-09 this is enforced at the attention chokepoint itself: `createAttentionItem` defaults to single-topic routing, posting EVERY item — HIGH/URGENT included — into the ONE "🔔 Attention" hub topic (per-item topics survive only behind the `attentionRouting.mode: 'per-item'` legacy opt-out). A feature that notifies per-element over a collection must AGGREGATE (one summary item carrying the count + the list), never one item per element. The bounding machinery beneath remains: `TelegramAdapter.createForumTopic` enforces a last-resort budget on every automatically-created topic (`origin: 'auto'` by default — the layer that cannot be dodged by giving each item a unique source, the fix after the 2026-06-05 worktree-detector flood); `AttentionTopicGuard` coalesces non-critical items from a flooding source in legacy mode; and `notification-flood-burst-invariant.test.ts` holds the bound at build time.

**Earned from.** The recurring topic-spam floods, all the same shape — a housekeeping feature spawning one topic per event: the 2026-05-22 sentinel flood, the 2026-05-28 collaboration-redrive flood, and the 2026-06-05 worktree-detector flood (which dodged the per-source budget by giving every item a unique source — the reason the ceiling had to move to the create primitive itself). A standing operator rule since 2026-07-01 ("user alerts go to ONE hub topic; NEVER create per-item or per-event topics"); operator-directed to constitutional status 2026-07-03 (topic 29723): "we have a history of creating new Telegram topics for every alert, which creates spam and noise topics; any message sent to the user that didn't belong to a topic needs to go to the dedicated alerts topic; this needs to be a standard."

**Traces to the goal.** The operator's attention and their Telegram surface are scarce and easily wrecked. A wall of noise topics is the same failure as paging for every hiccup (**Self-Heal Before Notify**) — it drowns the signal the operator actually needs. This is the routing corollary of **Bounded Notification Surface**: that standard caps how MANY topics may be born; this one names WHERE an ownerless notice goes instead — the ONE alerts topic, by construction.

**Applied through.** The single-topic default at the routing chokepoint itself — `TelegramAdapter.createAttentionItem` routes every item into the "🔔 Attention" hub unless `attentionRouting.mode: 'per-item'` opts out (shipped 2026-07-09; the enforcement build this entry previously tracked); the `topicCreationBudget` ceiling inside `TelegramAdapter.createForumTopic` (origin-typed, auto-by-default) + `AttentionTopicGuard` at `createAttentionItem` + `tests/integration/notification-flood-burst-invariant.test.ts` — the existing *Bounded Notification Surface* machinery, which this standard makes load-bearing for routing. This registry entry + its **P23** twin in `docs/INSTAR-DESIGN-PRINCIPLES-AND-LESSONS.md` fire the `/spec-converge` lessons-aware reviewer on any feature that would create a topic per alert.

