./upgrades/side-effects/threadline-cooldown-sec4.1-receiver-affinity.md:20:1. **Evict-on-read vs periodic sweep.** Spec §4.1 mentions "periodic sweep every 5 min". Evict-on-read is functionally equivalent for correctness, requires no timer plumbing, and has no dispose() lifecycle. Chose evict-on-read; sweep can be added in a later commit if adversarial churn causes stale-entry pressure.
./docs/specs/reports/durable-inbound-message-queue-xrev/xrev-codex-r2.json:1:{"status":"ok","framework":"codex-cli","model":"gpt-5.5","verdict":"MINOR ISSUES","findings":[{"reviewer":"cross-model:codex-cli:gpt-5.5","verdict":"MINOR ISSUES","body":"**Verdict: MINOR ISSUES**\n\n1. **§3.5 Lease/topology gating may convert recoverable custody into loss too eagerly.**  \n   The drain only runs on the router lease holder, but custody is stored on the machine that accepted the message. If the router lease moves, local rows age out with `lease-moved-before-drain`. That is honest, but architecturally odd: the new router cannot drain the old router’s local SQLite, and the old router is forbidden to drain what it already safely owns. This creates avoidable loss during benign planned handoff. Resolution: either allow a non-router custody holder to drain only rows whose session ownership still validates, or explicitly justify why stale replay risk dominates recoverable delivery. The planned-handoff test currently expects expiry, which bakes in this loss posture.\n\n2. **§1 / §2 `maxTotal` refusal fallback can still violate the stated ordering invariant.**  \n   The spec says a refused enqueue falls back to today’s immediate local delivery. If the refusal happens while that session has claimed/non-terminal predecessors, the fallback can leapfrog or wrong-place deliver. The global cap carve-out helps sessions already queued, but refusal can still occur from size, store error, negative cache, or first-entry cap. Resolution: split refusal causes: some may fail open, but ordering-relevant failures should probably fail closed with an explicit loss report or bounded platform-visible delay.\n\n3. **§3.4 idempotency claims are underspecified for local delivery.**  \n   “Records the receipt atomically with the row’s delivered transition” crosses two durability domains: receipt store, queue SQLite row, and possibly PendingInjectStore. Unless these share one transaction, this is not atomic. The spec gestures at settle ordering but not exact recovery states for every partial failure. Resolution: define a small state machine/table of crash points: receipt written/row not delivered, inject recorded/receipt absent, inject completed/receipt write fails, etc.\n\n4. **§6 monotonic persistence is likely harder than described.**  \n   `enqueued_mono` cannot survive process restart as a meaningful absolute timestamp unless the monotonic source has a persisted translation layer. The spec mentions boot sweeps and stale clamps, but not how pre-reboot monotonic deadlines are reconstructed. Resolution: define persisted `{wall, mono}` calibration or store relative remaining TTL/backoff at shutdown/checkpoint; otherwise use wall time with wake compensation and bound the known skew.\n\n5. **Alternative design not fully acknowledged.**  \n   This is effectively a local durable inbox plus retry worker. Existing patterns include brokered queues, replicated logs, or using the platform offset as the authoritative replay mechanism. The non-goals reject platform-offset back-pressure, but the spec should add a short rationale for choosing local SQLite custody over fixing the already-existing ACK/offset contract, because that is the simpler conceptual source of truth.\n\nNo LLM-authority concern: the design is deterministic and appropriately non-LLM."}],"flag":"cross-model-review: codex-cli:gpt-5.5","promptTruncated":true}
./upgrades/side-effects/threadline-cooldown-sec4.2-infra-soft-limiter.md:30:5. **No DegradationReporter breadcrumb wired in this commit.** Spec mentions "spawn-infra-degraded" breadcrumb. DegradationReporter integration belongs in §4.5 observability — keeping this commit focused. The behavior (cap on queue) is the load-bearing part.
./docs/specs/reports/relay-spawn-ghost-reply-containment-convergence.md:21:3. **Reply provenance.** Replies that talk about the codebase have to be backed by a record of commands the session actually ran (captured by a wrapper around `git`/`gh`/etc). Replies that mention five commit hashes but the wrapper only saw one `git log` get quarantined for the user to look at, never auto-forwarded.
